I record and cite published UK sources rather than advise or recommend. I am not able to tell you which form of magnesium to buy — that would require a personal recommendation, which is outside what this service does. What I can do is set out what authoritative UK sources state about magnesium, including the forms that appear in UK prescribing references, so you or a clinician can review the evidence.

---

**Magnesium** is a mineral that, according to the NHS, helps turn food into energy and ensures the parathyroid glands — which produce hormones important for bone health — work normally. The NHS also describes it as a metallic element mainly found in the bones and essential to life, while NHS (Hull University Teaching Hospitals) notes it is important for many systems in the body, especially muscles and nerves. NICE (NG154) further states that magnesium is required for energy transfer, storage and use, protein, carbohydrate and lipid metabolism, cell membrane function, regulation of parathyroid hormone secretion, and is involved in the development of the skeletal, nervous, and muscle membrane systems.

The NHS states that good food sources include spinach, nuts, and wholemeal bread. NHS (Leeds Teaching Hospitals) identifies wholegrain breakfast cereals, wholemeal bread, sardines, and cheese as among the best food sources, with white bread and yeast extract listed as additional sources. NHS (Lancashire Teaching Hospitals) also lists baked beans, various beans, yoghurt, halibut, tuna, bananas, avocados, bran, brown rice, dark chocolate, almonds, cashews, pumpkin seeds, and green leafy vegetables, among others.

The NHS states the UK reference intake for adults aged 19 to 64 is 300 mg per day for men and 270 mg per day for women. The NHS also states that taking more than 400 mg per day from supplements for a short time can cause diarrhoea, and that there is not enough evidence to say what the effects of taking high doses for a long time might be; it further states that 400 mg or less per day from supplements is unlikely to cause any harm.

Regarding forms: NICE (ESUOM4), citing the BNF, states that magnesium glycerophosphate tablets or liquid are suitable — though unlicensed — preparations for the prevention of recurrent hypomagnesaemia, available from special-order manufacturers or specialist importing companies. The BNF lists magnesium sulfate, magnesium citrate, and magnesium glycerophosphate as recognised prescribable preparations in the UK. No statement was found from NHS.uk, NICE, or the BNF specifically addressing the regulatory status or comparative suitability of magnesium forms sold as over-the-counter dietary supplements.

