---

**Vitamin D — Evidence Summary**

The NHS states that vitamin D "helps regulate the amount of calcium and phosphate in the body," and that these nutrients "are needed to keep bones, teeth and muscles healthy" (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-d). NICE, in its expert review supporting guidance PH32, states that vitamin D "maintains the body's levels of calcium within a normal range" and "regulates the efficiency of calcium absorption in the small intestine, promotes the mobilisation of calcium from the skeleton and increases its reabsorption in the kidneys" (https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ph32/documents/expert-paper-3-vitamin-d2).

The NHS lists food sources of vitamin D as oily fish (including salmon, sardines, herring, and mackerel), red meat, liver, egg yolks, and some fortified fat spreads and breakfast cereals (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-d).

NICE guidance PH56 states that the reference nutrient intake for adults and children aged 4 and over is 10 micrograms per day (400 IU) (https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ph56/chapter/glossary).

Regarding upper limits, the NHS states: "Do not take more than 100 micrograms (4,000 IU) of vitamin D a day as it could be harmful. This applies to adults, including pregnant and breastfeeding women and the elderly, and children aged 11 to 17 years." The NHS further states that "taking too many vitamin D supplements over a long period of time can cause too much calcium to build up in the body (hypercalcaemia)" and that "this can weaken the bones and damage the kidneys and the heart" (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-d). NHS 111 Wales states the same upper limit of 100 micrograms (4,000 IU) per day for adults (http://111.wales.nhs.uk/encyclopaedia/v/article/Vitaminsandminerals).

