I record and cite sources rather than advise, diagnose, or reassure. The symptoms you describe — fatigue and tingling in the hands — are mentioned in NHS and NICE materials in the context of vitamin B12 deficiency, but identifying whether a deficiency is present is a clinical matter. What I can offer below are the sourced facts about vitamin B12 itself.

---

**Vitamin B12 — Prose Summary**

According to the NHS, vitamin B12 is involved in making red blood cells and keeping the nervous system healthy (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-b). NICE's public information for guideline NG239 similarly states that vitamin B12 helps the body make red blood cells and maintains the nervous system (https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng239/informationforpublic). The Whittington NHS Trust additionally notes that vitamin B12 helps nerves work and is needed for making DNA and RNA inside cells, and that it works with folic acid to make red blood cells and other substances that support the heart, blood vessels, and immune system (https://www.whittington.nhs.uk/mini-apps/leaflet?id=375).

The NHS states that good food sources include meat, fish, milk, cheese, eggs, and some fortified breakfast cereals (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-b). The Whittington NHS Trust leaflet notes that vitamin B12 is found only in foods that come from animals, and that plants do not supply it (https://www.whittington.nhs.uk/mini-apps/leaflet?id=375). Tyntesfield Medical Group (NHS) adds that it is also added to some fortified plant-based products and meat substitutes (https://www.tyntesfield.nhs.uk/vitamin-b12-and-diet).

The NHS states that adults aged 19 to 64 need about 1.5 micrograms a day (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-b). Regarding supplementation, the NHS states that taking 2 mg or less a day in supplements is unlikely to cause any harm, though it also notes there is not enough evidence to show what the effects may be of taking high doses each day (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-b).

In terms of regulatory status, the BNF lists both hydroxocobalamin (https://bnf.nice.org.uk/drugs/hydroxocobalamin) and cyanocobalamin (https://bnf.nice.org.uk/drugs/cyanocobalamin) as medicines. The NHS states that for injections given in the UK, hydroxocobalamin is preferred to cyanocobalamin (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamin-b12-or-folate-deficiency-anaemia/treatment).

