**Melatonin — Evidence Summary**

Melatonin is a hormone produced naturally by the pineal gland in the brain. The NHS states that it "helps control your sleep cycle," with the body producing it after dark, peaking in the early hours of the morning, and reducing during daylight hours, acting on receptors in the body to encourage sleep (https://www.nhs.uk/medicines/melatonin/common-questions-about-melatonin). NICE describes melatonin as "involved in regulating sleep and circadian rhythms," and its evidence review notes that it synchronises the internal body clock to the light-dark cycle, has a sedative effect, and acts on melatonin receptors MT1, MT2, and MT3 (https://www.nice.org.uk/advice/es38/evidence/evidence-review-pdf-9202622509).

Regarding the question of over-the-counter availability in the UK: the NHS states plainly that melatonin "is available on prescription only" and that, although melatonin supplements are sold as complementary medicines in some countries and online, "these supplements are not authorised for sale in the UK and it's a prescription-only medicine" (https://www.nhs.uk/medicines/melatonin/about-melatonin; https://www.nhs.uk/medicines/melatonin/common-questions-about-melatonin). No UK authority source retrieved indicates that melatonin is available without a prescription in the UK.

No UK population-level reference intake exists for melatonin; UK sources treat it exclusively as a medicine. NHS-affiliated patient resources mention cherries, cherry juice, bananas, and milk in the context of foods associated with melatonin or sleep-related compounds, though these references originate from NHS Trust patient information pages rather than the main NHS medicines pages, NICE, or the BNF. The maximum prescribed dose cited by the NHS is 10 mg once daily, stated in a clinical prescribing context rather than as a population-level tolerable upper intake.

